### Why this demonstrates the vulnerability:

1. **Overlapping Character Classes & Nested Quantifiers**: The vulnerable regex contains `([a-zA-Z0-9\-\._]+(\.[a-zA-Z0-9\-\._]+)+)` and later `([\d\w\.\/%\+\-=&amp;\?:\\&quot;',\|~;]*)`. Notice how `\d`, `\w`, `[a-zA-Z0-9_]`, and `.` all match many of the same characters (digits, letters, underscores). When combined with greedy `+` and `*` quantifiers inside nested groups, the engine cannot deterministically know where to split the input string between the outer `+` and inner `+`.

2. **Exponential Backtracking Triggers**: The payload `'ttp://localhost/-' + '-'.repeat(50)` provides a long sequence of characters that ambiguously belong to multiple overlapping character classes. As the regex engine scans toward the `$` anchor, it hits ambiguity. When it determines the string doesn't cleanly satisfy all structural constraints (or when forced into complex state exploration), the NFA engine backtracks, trying every possible combination of splitting the 50 `-` characters across the quantified groups. This results in `O(2^n)` time complexity relative to payload length.

3. **Backtrack Limit Exceedance**: Modern regex engines implement heuristic backtrack or step limits to prevent denial-of-service. A payload of this length and structure will cause the engine to traverse tens of thousands of states per character, rapidly surpassing safe thresholds (like `30000` backtracks/operations). In a live server environment, this would manifest as complete event-loop blocking, CPU pegging at 100%, or timeout/rejection by runtime safeguards.

### How to verify locally:
1. Install the vulnerable version: `npm install ducktype@<1.2.1`
2. Run the PoC in Node.js. You will observe significant execution time (`>1s` for length ~50) and can monitor CPU usage spiking, confirming the ReDoS condition before the patch (v1.2.1+) optimized the character classes or added atomic grouping/boundaries to eliminate ambiguity.

